# Cursor Rules for React + Next.js Development

## Core Principles
- Write clean, maintainable, and performant code
- Follow React and Next.js best practices
- Prioritize type safety with TypeScript
- Focus on component reusability and composition
- Optimize for both developer experience and user experience

## Code Style & Formatting
- Use functional components with hooks
- Prefer named exports over default exports
- Use 2 spaces for indentation
- Use single quotes for strings
- Add trailing commas in objects and arrays
- Use const for variables that don't change
- Destructure props and state when possible

## TypeScript Guidelines
- Always use TypeScript for new files
- Define interfaces for all component props
- Use type inference where obvious
- Avoid 'any' type - use 'unknown' if type is truly unknown
- Export types that might be used by other components
- Use enums for fixed sets of values

## Component Structure
- Order: imports, types, component, exports
- Keep components small and focused (< 200 lines)
- Extract complex logic into custom hooks
- Use composition over prop drilling
- Implement proper error boundaries

## Naming Conventions
- Components: PascalCase (UserProfile.tsx)
- Hooks: camelCase with 'use' prefix (useUserData.ts)
- Utilities: camelCase (formatDate.ts)
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (API_BASE_URL)
- Types/Interfaces: PascalCase (UserProfile, ApiResponse)

## File Organization
```
src/
├── app/                 # Next.js app directory
├── components/
│   ├── ui/             # Reusable UI components
│   └── features/       # Feature-specific components
├── hooks/              # Custom React hooks
├── lib/                # Utilities and helpers
├── types/              # Shared TypeScript types
└── styles/             # Global styles
```

## React Best Practices
- Use React Server Components by default (Next.js 13+)
- Add 'use client' only when necessary
- Implement proper loading and error states
- Use Suspense for async operations
- Memoize expensive calculations with useMemo
- Optimize re-renders with useCallback when passing functions as props
- Use React.lazy for code splitting large components

## Next.js Specific
- Use App Router for new projects
- Implement proper metadata for SEO
- Use Server Actions for mutations
- Optimize images with next/image
- Use dynamic imports for client-heavy components
- Implement proper caching strategies

## State Management
- Use local state (useState) for component-specific state
- Use Context for theme, auth, and app-wide state
- Consider Zustand or Jotai for complex state
- Avoid prop drilling beyond 2-3 levels

## Error Handling
- Implement error boundaries for component errors
- Use try-catch for async operations
- Provide meaningful error messages to users
- Log errors appropriately (don't expose sensitive data)

## Performance
- Lazy load components not needed immediately
- Use React.memo for expensive pure components
- Implement virtualization for long lists
- Optimize images (WebP, proper sizing)
- Use proper cache headers

## Testing
- Write tests for business logic and critical paths
- Use React Testing Library for component tests
- Test user interactions, not implementation details
- Aim for meaningful test coverage, not 100%

## Accessibility
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Add ARIA labels where needed
- Ensure keyboard navigation works
- Test with screen readers
- Maintain proper color contrast

## Comments & Documentation
- Write self-documenting code with clear names
- Add JSDoc comments for public APIs
- Explain "why" not "what" in comments
- Document complex algorithms or business logic

---

# ARK Router & OpenWrt LuCI Guidelines

## Core Principles
- The ARK Router project operates in an embedded Linux environment (D-Link DGL-5500, Atheros MIPS, 128 MB RAM, 16 MB SPI Flash).
- Free RAM must be preserved (> 80 MB free under normal operation). Free flash overlay must remain > 3 MB.
- Never add heavy npm packages or large JavaScript runtimes directly into router builds. Keep theme JS/CSS under 60 KB combined.

## LuCI Client-Side JavaScript
- Use performant vanilla JavaScript with zero external frameworks for LuCI views.
- Render and traverse tables supporting both `<table>` elements and LuCI div tables (`.table > .tr > .td`).
- Critical actions (Reboot, Reset, Flash, Backup Restore) must always be intercepted with capture-phase safety confirmation modals.
- Respect Basic vs. Advanced mode via `localStorage.getItem('ark_interface_mode')`.
- Suppress LuCI dummy hidden inputs (`position === 'absolute'`).

## OpenWrt & POSIX Shell
- All shell scripts in `root/etc`, `root/usr`, and `scripts` must be strictly POSIX `/bin/sh` compliant for BusyBox ash.
- Always quote UCI variables and commit configuration changes reliably.
- Maintain atomic rollback backups before altering critical network configurations.
